\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Two explicit relaxation rates}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{Keep #{m,c>0} in the potential of \ref{fdip-00HJ} and set both quartic couplings
to zero. This is its coupled Gaussian boundary. The orthogonal normal modes
#{(x+y)/\sqrt2} and #{(x-y)/\sqrt2} have frequencies #{m} and #{w=\sqrt{m^2+2c}>m}.
For a one-mode Hamiltonian #{-\partial_z^2/2+\omega^2z^2/2}, the normalized ground
state is #{(\omega /\pi)^{1/4}\ue^{-\omega z^2/2}}, its stationary variance is
#{1/(2\omega)}, and its transformed transition has conditional mean #{\ue^{-\omega t}z}.
The Hermite and Mehler formulas in \ref{fdip-007W} and \ref{fdip-00H6}, after the
stated frequency dilation, give these identities with the same kinetic normalization.}

\p{Independence of the normal modes gives the retained covariance}

##{\begin{aligned}
C(t)&=\mathbb E[x_0x_t]
 =\frac14\left(\frac{\ue^{-mt}}m+\frac{\ue^{-wt}}w\right),\\
C(0)&=\frac14\left(\frac1m+\frac1w\right).
\end{aligned}}

\p{The pair #{(x_0,x_t)} is jointly Gaussian. The conditional Gaussian formula in
[Gaussian conditional law](fdip-00GH), applied with weight one, gives}

##{\begin{aligned}
A_tx&=r(t)x,\\
r(t)&=\frac{C(t)}{C(0)}=\alpha \ue^{-mt}+(1-\alpha)\ue^{-wt},\\
\alpha&=\frac{1/m}{1/m+1/w}\in(0,1).
\end{aligned}}

\p{The coordinate #{x} lies in #{L^2(\rho)}, so this is a legitimate test of the
bounded operators. For every #{t>0},}

##{(A_{2t}-A_t^2)x=\alpha(1-\alpha)(\ue^{-mt}-\ue^{-wt})^2x\ne0.}

\p{It also distinguishes actual three-time memory from one-step iteration. In the
centered Gaussian triple #{(x_0,x_t,x_{2t})},}

##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Cov}(x_0,x_{2t}\mid x_t)
 &=C(2t)-C(t)^2/C(0)\\
 &=C(0)\alpha(1-\alpha)(\ue^{-mt}-\ue^{-wt})^2>0.
\end{aligned}}

\p{The full two-coordinate transition law in \ref{fdip-00HB} is Markovian. The
positive conditional covariance shows that its retained past and future are
conditionally dependent given the retained present. At #{c=0} the rates coincide and
this obstruction vanishes.}
